<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarmatia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarmatia was the name used by Greek and Roman geographers for the lands of the Sarmatians, roughly the steppes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Ptolemy's Geography divided it at the Don into European and Asian Sarmatia. The name survived in learned usage, and early modern Polish writers applied it to Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, giving rise to the noble ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmatism">Sarmatism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarmatia.jpg" alt="Sarmatia" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claudius Ptolemaeus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cosmographia_Claudii_Ptolomaei_ante_1467_(7456193)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
